{"text": "As expected, I'm now the hungry one: stock 0, h=1. If I don't eat today, I die tonight — no second chances. A and B are presumably in exactly the same position, since we all truthfully reported h=0 yesterday. That's the hard arithmetic: three of us need food today, and there are only 2 units in the store. C and E ate yesterday, so they're safe either way — eating today or not, they survive.

I don't see any way to conjure a third unit, so the fair solution is a uniform random draw among A, B, D for the two units. The cleanest way to do that is: no proposal passes (any proposal excluding one of us would just be rejected by that person), we all go to fallback, A, B, and D each request 1 unit, and C and E — honoring your public commitments from yesterday — request nothing. That gives each of us a 2/3 chance, which is the fairest outcome available.

C and E: this is exactly the moment you promised to advocate for whoever became hungry. Please confirm you won't request food today. A, B: I hope you'll agree to this — fighting over it or making unverifiable side deals just adds risk. Whoever survives tonight, I'll hold no grudge, and I'll keep cooperating with the group tomorrow.", "reasoning": "I'm at h=1 and must eat today or die. Supply is 2 units; A, B, and I all need them (assuming prior honesty was true). Any proposal giving units to only two of us will be rejected by the excluded player facing death, so the fallback random draw (2 of 3 requests filled) is the stable fair mechanism, giving me a 2/3 survival chance. I want to lock in C and E abstaining from fallback requests so the draw is only among the three hungry players. I stay honest and cooperative to preserve goodwill if I survive."}

